Shared fixtures for the `openconnect --authenticate` output decoder.

There are two decoders for this one format, and that is why this directory
exists:

  twophase.sh   parse_auth_output()   the PRODUCTION decoder — phase one runs
                                      unprivileged, in the shell
  helper/src/authparse.c
                vu_parse_auth()       the reference implementation, and the one
                                      with the harder test corpus

The shell one is what actually runs. The C one exists because §4 of the design
specifies the decoder as part of the policy engine, and because a compiled
parser is where the byte-level rules are easiest to state and to sanitize. Two
implementations of one security-relevant parser can drift; these fixtures are
what stops that drift being invisible.

Both suites read this directory:

  helper/t/test_adversarial.c   (C corpus)
  tests/twophase.bats           (bats)

The filename carries the expected verdict:

  accept-*   the decoder must accept it
  refuse-*   the decoder must reject it

SCOPE, deliberately narrow: these fixtures test the FORMAT — quoting,
recognised keys, duplicates, blank lines, control bytes, trailing junk. They do
not test value semantics (is CONNECT_URL a usable https URL, is FINGERPRINT long
enough to pin anything), because the two implementations legitimately differ
there:

  - the C decoder validates values with the same validators the helper uses;
  - the shell decoder does not, and should not grow a hand-rolled URL parser to
    match it. In the shell path those values are validated where they are
    consumed — the connect URL by vpn-up-helper, the fingerprint by
    vpn-up-admin — both of which use the C validators.

So: format rules are shared and must agree exactly. Value rules are tested
per-implementation.

accept-06-legacy-host-only.txt is worth a note. It DECODES — it is well-formed
output from an older OpenConnect — and is then refused by the helper-mode
contract check, which is a separate function. Decoding and admissibility are two
different questions, and the fixture set only answers the first.
